  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video is sponsored by Opera. More about them \nat the end. When I took this photo, I didn't know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what I was looking at. It seemed like a rainbow \nexcept with all of its color drained away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a shadow or a ghost. Ghost rainbows like \nthis are technically called fog bows. And fog  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bows form in almost the exact same way as regular \nrainbows, except for one key difference. They only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form around Halloween. Uh, no, it's actually \nthat the water droplets are around 10 to 100 or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23520,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more times smaller than those in a rainbow. And \nthat's what makes all the diffraction. I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the difference. So, let's get into how smaller \ndroplets remove the color from a rainbow. There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31840,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are some great in-depth explanations for rainbows, \nso we won't get into the details here, but the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relevant summary is this. When sunlight strikes \na drop of water, some of it reflects backwards  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the insides of the drop at a bunch of angles, \nbut most brightly at around 140°. The exact angle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44480,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on the color, with bluer light bending \nthe most and ending up closest to 140°, while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48320,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "redder light bends the least. The result is that \nwhite light gets spread out into a rainbow-like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52160,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum of colors when it reflects off the inside \nof a raindrop. So, if you look up at the sky at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55920,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a collection of drops, when you look up at an \nangle of 42 degrees relative to the direction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the sun, you'll see redder light coming from \nthose drops. And when you look at 40° relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sun, you'll see bluer light with the other \nrainbow colors coming from drops in between.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there you have it, a nice rainbow, except \nlight is a wave, and waves can interfere with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70240,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other and themselves, which messes up the \nperfect light ray picture we just presented. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73920,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the case of the reflected light from a raindrop, \nthe physics is similar to what happens when you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77200,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shine a light through a slit. Since a reflection \noff of a small object isn't that different than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80800,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sending light through an opening, the size of the \nobject from the opposite direction. With a slit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84720,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference happens because waves passing through \ndifferent parts of the slit alternately add up or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancel out and create what's called a diffraction \npattern of bright and dark spots. And with a small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92480,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object, it's the reflections off the different \nparts of the object that add up and cancel out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The effect on rainbows is that instead of a drop \njust reflecting back bright light at around 140°,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100320,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are a bunch more bright reflections at more \nextreme angles. And this means that each color  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't just appear as a single ring, but instead \na bright outer ring and a progressively fainter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "series of concentric inner rings. The reason we \ndon't see concentric rainbows is because of a key  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "property of diffraction patterns. A wide slit will \nhave lots of narrow, closely spaced bright spots,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while a narrow slit will have wider, farther \napart peaks. The narrower the slit, the broader  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the diffraction pattern. The wider the slit, the \ntighter the pattern. And the raindrops in normal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rainbows are the equivalent of very wide slits. \nFor a rainbow formed from raindrops a millimeter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or larger in size, the diffraction pattern is \nso narrow that the effects of diffraction are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially absent, and you get the crisp, \nhigh contrast stereotypical rainbow. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for drops a bit smaller than a millimeter, the \ndiffraction pattern creates concentric rings for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141840,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each color that are far enough apart to create \nextra repeating colored arcs on the inside of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the original rainbow. These are so-called \nsupernumemerary bows. With even smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water droplets around a 20th of a millimeter, the \nrainbow and supernumerary bows get even broader  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even farther apart. But crucially, different \ncolors of light are still only bent away from each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other by the same amount as before. And so the \nbroadening of the rings causes colors to blur  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into each other, making the rainbow less vivid. \nEventually, with super small water droplets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few hundredths of a millimeter, like you get in \nfog, diffraction causes all the individual colors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171040,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to broaden out so much they pretty much completely \noverlap and add back up to the same original color  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sunlight as they started. White light. You \ncan see the same thing happen if you look at red,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "green, and blue colors through an out of focus \nlens. They blur together into white. A fog bow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183440,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is still basically a rainbow caused by the \nsame physics, but it's one that's had the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "color blurred out of it by diffraction from the \ntiny fog droplets. P.S. If you take just a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slice from a rainbow and plot how the rainbow \nchanges as the water drop size gets smaller,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get a really cool diagram. On one end, \nwe have slices corresponding to the purest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colored rainbows, and on the other end, slices \ncorresponding to pure white fog bows with all the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other varieties of rainbows with supernumemerary \nbows in between. It's a true opera of colors,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which brings us to today's sponsor, Opera. When I \nfirst saw this phenomenon years ago out on a walk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213520,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was baffled and had to scour the internet to \nfigure out what it was. If instead I could have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217280,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uploaded the photo to Opera's image recognition \ntool, I would have immediately seen it. that it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a fog bow and gotten a quick overview right \nthere, as well as a deep dive via the links  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "provided. Deep diving is also assisted by Opera's \ntab islands. You can neatly group papers, videos,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and searches into separate tab islands, keeping \neverything organized while still leaving space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for your email. And tab traces keep track of \nwhere you've been most recently. The darker the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237520,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underscore, the more recent the visit, so you \ncan more easily keep track of the tab you were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just on. Plus, Opera's pop out video player lets \nyou watch videos while you browse, which is great  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for following along with tutorials or how-to \nvideos. So, if you're inquisitive, creative,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or love to multitask, then try Opera. You may \ndiscover a new essential tool for web browsing."
    } ]
  } ]
}
